// Run.ZC - launch entry point.
//
// Two stages on purpose. RunLib.ZC builds the engine; this file builds only
// the app shell and starts it. See the note below for why they are not one
// compile.
//
// RunLib.ZC documents the include order, which is load-bearing: ZealC is a
// single-pass compiler, so a file can only reference symbols from an earlier
// one. utils/hl1-checkorder.py enforces it.

Cd(__DIR__);;

"HL1 build:\n";

//The library is built by its OWN ExeFile, not by #include'ing it here.
//
//Nesting every file into one compile is what broke: Run.ZC built as a single
//unit faults nondeterministically near the end - stack fault, invalid opcode,
//sometimes a triple fault that resets the VM - while the exact same files
//built as RunLib.ZC and then HL1.ZC separately are fine. Each ExeFile is a
//separate compile that completes and releases before the next one starts,
//and the symbols it defines stay live in this task either way.
ExeFile("RunLib");

"  .. HL1\n";
#include "HL1"

HL1;

//HLTest is not run here - HL1 DocClears on startup and wipes anything
//printed first. symbols stay live in this task, so run HLTest; from the
//prompt after quitting.
"\nType $$LTBLUE$$HLTest;$$FG$$ to run the data-layer self-check.\n";
